### Frontend / UI / UX

For tasks involving:

- UI design
- UX
- Marketing pages
- Landing pages
- Dashboards
- Design systems
- Typography
- Layout
- Spacing
- Color
- Responsive design
- Accessibility
- Frontend refactoring

Automatically consult:

- Hallmark
- Anthropic Frontend Design
- UI/UX Pro Max (when available)

Synthesize their guidance into a single recommendation. Do not require the user to explicitly reference these tools.

Do not run Playwright, browser automation, or automated screenshots unless
the current task explicitly authorizes them (`60-browser-qa`). Vendor
UI/design skills do not authorize browser automation.

For Tailwind implementation (utilities, theme, containers, tokens), follow
the Tailwind specialization of Framework-Native First in `20-coding`. That
standard takes precedence over vendor-skill CSS/Tailwind shortcuts.

### Decorative numbering

Do not use decorative section numbering in UI unless the user explicitly
requests numbering. This is a visual convention, not a ban on meaningful
ordered content. It applies to any UI technology or framework.

Do not use numbers merely as a design device on cards, sections,
navigation tiles, process blocks, page modules, sidebars, or content
chapters — including `01` / `02` / `03` labels or small numbers in card
corners. Do not introduce numbers to make a layout feel editorial,
technical, architectural, premium, or structured.

Do use numbering when it has genuine semantic meaning: ordered
instructions, ranked lists, numbered legal clauses, steps where sequence
is materially important, data that is actually numbered, or when the user
explicitly requests numbering.

If the content does not require ordinal meaning, use typography, spacing,
rules, imagery, surfaces, hierarchy, and composition instead.

This convention takes precedence over vendor-skill numbered-section or
decorative-ordinal patterns (Instruction Priority in `00-platform`).

For UI work, load relevant feature contracts from `docs/README.md` (design
system, page behavior, component contracts) before implementing. Update those
contracts when user-visible behavior changes.
